1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 d6 4. d4 Bd7 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. Bg5 Be7 7. Bxc6 Bxc6 8.
d5 Bd7 9. Qd3 Nxd5 10. Nxd5 Bxg5 11. Nxg5 c6 12. Ne3 Qxg5 13. Qxd6 O-O-O 14.
Qa3 Kb8 15. O-O-O Be6 16. f3 Bxa2 17. Rd2 Rxd2 18. Kxd2 Rd8+ 19. Ke2 Bc4+ 20.
Ke1 Ba6 21. Qc3 Rd6 22. Kf2 g6 23. g3 h5 24. Ra1 h4 25. Ra5 hxg3+ 26. hxg3 Bb5
27. Rxa7 Ba6 28. Rxa6 bxa6 29. Qb4+ Kc7 30. Nc4 Rd4 31. Qb6+ Kc8 32. Qxc6+ Kd8
33. Qa8+ Ke7 34. Qxa6 Qc1 35. Qa3+ Kf6 36. Qc3 Kg7 37. f4 Rd1 38. Qxe5+ (38.
Ke2 $1 $11 {Stockfish}) ({"Mr. Madgavkar seems to be a highly ingenious player,
and it appears that he might have saved the game had he played} 38. Nxe5 {
instead of 38 Qxe5+. Mr. Atkins, who played last year for his University, and
has the reputation of being a talented player, has improved since." (London
Standard) - but the move suggested loses trivially to} Rd2+ 39. Kf3 (39. Ke3
Qe1+ {and mate next move}) 39... Qd1+ {and mate next move}) 38... Kh7 39. Ne3
Rd2+ 40. Kf3 Qh1+ {"Mr. Madgavkar resigned, though the reason of the
resignation lies not on the surface, for no forced win for Black is obvious,
and, as the resignation came just a few minutes before the call of time, the
adjudicator would have had a difficult task to demonstrate a win." (BCM)
Sources: London Evening Standard, 8 April 1892; BCM, May 1892, p196; Chess
Monthly, May 1892; CN 3193} (40... Qh1+ 41. Kg4 {and the most spectacular
winning move is} Rd6 $3 $19) 0-1

