1. d4 f5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 b6 5. Bf4 Bb7 6. e3 Bb4 7. a3 Bxc3+ 8. bxc3
O-O 9. Bd3 d6 10. h3 Qe7 11. Bh2 Ne4 12. Qc2 Nd7 13. O-O Kh8 14. Ne1 Qg5 15.
Bf4 Qg6 16. f3 Ng5 17. Kh2 e5 18. Bxg5 Qxg5 19. f4 exf4 20. Rxf4 g6 21. Qf2
Rae8 22. Nf3 Qh6 23. Re1 Nf6 24. Rh4 Qg7 25. Ng5 Be4 26. Bxe4 fxe4 27. Qc2 Qe7
28. g3 h5 29. Rf4 Ng4+ 30. hxg4 Qxg5 31. Ref1 Rxf4 32. Rxf4 hxg4 33. Qe2 Kg7
34. Qxg4 Qxg4 35. Rxg4 c6 36. Rg5 Kf6 37. Rg4 g5 {Source: Chelmsford Chronicle,
2 March 1900, p2, which identifies Black as Edmund Wood, "... securing a
brilliant victory over the celebrated international, Mr. H. E. Atkins". On
page 5 of the same edition, there is further detail: "Mr Edmund Wood, of the
Essex Association, a young and comparatively little known player, beat no less
a person than Mr. H. E. Atkins, the amateur champion." I am grateful to Gerard
Killoran for unearthing the above references, and also for subsequent research
which seems to indicate that the player was Edmund John Wood, born abt 1876, s.
of Joseph Wood, marrying Anne Louisa Formby on 26 Dec 1903 at Waltham Holy
Cross, St Lawrence and Holy Cross, Essex, England. Gerard found another
reference to a game that Edmund Wood lost and then nothing more. I took up the
quest and found that Wood was a storeman for the Minister of Supply and lived
in Enfield with his wife and family at the time of the 1911 and 1939 censuses.
His d.o.b. appears to be 24 August 1876, acc. the 1939 census, but the entry
there introduces a doubt as to his first name - a census worker at some point
in the past has crossed out 'Edmund' and written instead 'Edward John'. He had
a son born 1908 also called Edmund J Wood.} 0-1

