1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. c3 d5 {('P-Q3' but must be wrong)} 4. Qa4 f6 5. Bb5 Nge7
6. exd5 Qxd5 7. O-O Bd7 8. d4 e4 9. Ne1 Ne5 10. Bxd7+ Nxd7 11. f3 f5 12. Be3 b5
{This is rather novel, but the move seems to protect the Black Queen against
disturbance tor the time being. Considering the undeveloped state of Black's
King's side, White should soon obtain an attack in that direction; wherefore
the transference of his Queen to the King's wing should be of advantage to him.
} 13. Qc2 Ng6 (13... Nf6 {for choice.}) 14. b3 c6 15. Nd2 Nf6 16. Bg5 c5 ({
Black has dallied too long and his game is hopelessly compromised. If} 16...
exf3 17. Bxf6 fxg2 18. Rxf5 {with a winning game.}) 17. Bxf6 e3 18. Bg5 cxd4
19. cxd4 Qxd4 {Black is making a desperate lunge, but his opponent's parry is
much too clever for him.} 20. Qc6+ Kf7 21. Nc2 Qe5 (21... Qxd2 22. Rad1 Qa5 23.
Qd5+ Ke8 24. Qd7#) 22. Bxe3 Bd6 23. f4 Qe6 24. Nf3 Rac8 25. Ng5+ Ke7 26. Qxb5
Qg8 27. Nd4 Nh4 28. Rfe1 Kf6 29. Bf2 Rc5 30. Qd7 Bxf4 31. Bxh4 Kg6 32. Nde6 Be5
33. Nxc5 {Source: Pall Mall Gazette, via Nottinghamshire Guardian, 13 October
1900 (n.b. the newspaper gives Black's name as "J Rumboll" but other
references to the tournament have "A Rumboll")} 1-0

