{[%mdl 32768] We know that the game opened with 1.d4 but we have no further
moves until this position after Black's 38th move, where White is under some
pressure:} 39. Qf6+ Kg8 40. Qf4 $4 ({A fatal repetition.} 40. Qf2 {was
slightly better, though, after} Ra7 41. Ke1 Ra1+ 42. Rd1 Rxd1+ 43. Kxd1 {
Black could try} Qd3+ 44. Kc1 g5 {with good winning chances.}) (40. Qh4 {
looks best, threatening a perpetual on d8 and f6 should the black rook leave
the d-file. Then} Qf1+ 41. Qe1 Qf5 {maintains the pressure on White.}) 40...
Ra7 $1 {and now the finish seems to have been as follows:} 41. Ke1 Ra1+ 42. Rd1
Rxd1+ 43. Kxd1 Qc2+ 44. Ke1 Qxb2 45. Qh4 Qc1+ 46. Ke2 g5 {Sources: The
Scotsman, 8 Aug 1906; London Evening Standard, 8 Aug 1906; annotations by JS,
CHESS, Aug 2017, p36} 0-1

