1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 d6 ({Not very
common at this stage, and probably inferior to the more usual} 6... b5) 7.
Bxc6+ bxc6 8. d4 Nd7 9. dxe5 dxe5 10. Nbd2 O-O ({In a game against Lasker 
(Match, 1910), Janowski here played} 10... f6 {and evolved a defence for his
c6-pawn (which the d2-N threatens to attack) by ...a5 and ...Ra6. But it was
not good.}) 11. Nc4 Bf6 12. Na5 Qe8 ({The alternative to giving up the c6-pawn
is} 12... Nb8 {. The objections to this are numerous. White exchanges queens
and after Be3 has an obvious grip on the queen's side. We have had the
advantage of Mr Atkins' considered opinion on the game, and he thinks that
giving up the pawn cannot be sound. Some time, however, is lost by White's
moves with the N. (Y)}) 13. Nxc6 Nb6 14. Nb4 Bb7 15. b3 ({If now} 15. Nd5 Bxd5
16. exd5 Rd8 {(Y)}) ({Better, perhaps, would be} 15. Nd3 {at once, to prevent
Black's 16th move; b3, played afterwards, would then threaten Ba3.}) 15... Rd8
{Necessary to prevent White fixing the N at d5. (Y)} 16. Qe2 Be7 17. Nd3 ({if} 
17. Nxa6 Bd6 {, threatening ...Ra8, to which there does not appear to be a
good reply. (Y)}) ({Interesting complications, worth examining, might arise
from} 17. Nd5 Nxd5 18. exd5 {; but they tend in favour of Black, whose best
line would seem to be} Rxd5 {(if} 19. Nxe5 Bg5 {)}) 17... f5 18. Nd2 ({If} 18.
exf5 {then} e4 {followed by ...Rxf5 with a fine game.}) 18... fxe4 19. Nxe4 Qg6
20. f3 ({Black's e-pawn is indirectly defended, for if} 20. Nxe5 Qxe4 21. Qxe4
Bxe4 {and White cannot retake owing to the mate on d1. (Y)}) 20... Bd6 21. Kh1
{Preparing the move Nxd6, to which, as the position stands, Black would have
the rejoinder Bxf3. Mr. Atkins considers he should have developed his bishop
somewhere at any one of the moves 21, 22 or 23, and that at move 23 he had two
moder­ately satisfactory moves, and two dangerous moves, of which he adopted
the worst. (Y.)} Nd5 22. c4 Nf6 23. Nxd6 ({This abandons control of e4, which
is practically a key-square. White has to provide against the immediate threat
of 23...Nxe4 24 dxe4 Bxe4; (if) 25 Qxe4 QXe4, etc. Best seems to be} 23. Bb2)
23... cxd6 24. Nf2 e4 25. f4 ({The obvious move} 25. Nxe4 {would have given
Black an even greater advantage by} Nxe4 26. fxe4 Rde8 {(Y)}) 25... e3 26. Nh3
({A better defence might be afforded by} 26. Nd1 {(if} Ng4 27. Bxe3 {; but
Black’s attack is very strong in any case. He now reaps the reward of an
admirably con­ducted plan of campaign, evolved from a somewhat speculative
opening.} ({Better than} 27. Nxe3)) 26... Ng4 27. Rf1 Nf2+ 28. Rxf2 exf2 29.
Qxf2 Rde8 30. Bd2 ({If} 30. Ba3 {, to avoid the continuation in the text, then}
Qg4 {, threatening ...Qxh3 or ...Re2.}) 30... Bxg2+ 31. Qxg2 Qxg2+ 32. Kxg2
Re2+ 33. Kg3 Rxd2 34. Ng5 Re8 35. Nf3 Re3 36. f5 Rdd3 37. Rf1 Kf7 38. Rf2 Kf6
39. Kf4 Re7 40. Rc2 g5+ 41. fxg6 hxg6 42. h4 Kg7 43. Rf2 Kh6 44. Kg4 Re4+ 45.
Kg3 Kh5 46. Rh2 Rg4+ 47. Kf2 Rc3 48. Rh3 Rf4 {The winning method is to
cap­ture the a-pawn, and then ex­change both rooks for R and N. The h-pawn
then falls and Black should be able to queen his g-pawn. (Y) Source: BCM,
January 1920, p23. "Played in a recent match between Bradford and Huddersfield.
Notes marked (Y) are by F. D. Yates in the Yorkshire Weekly Post." (However,
BCM is wrong about Yates' team - he was playing for Leeds in the Woodhouse Cup
- see Sheffield Daily Telegraph - Monday 15 December 1919)} 0-1

