1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 d5 3. c4 c6 4. Nc3 dxc4 5. e3 b5 6. a4 b4 7. Na2 e6 8. Bxc4
Nbd7 9. O-O Be7 10. Bd2 a5 11. Qe2 O-O 12. Rfd1 Ne4 13. Be1 Qb6 14. Rac1 Bb7
15. Qc2 c5 16. b3 cxd4 17. exd4 Rac8 18. Qe2 Rfd8 19. Rc2 Ndf6 20. Qe3 Bd6 21.
h3 Bb8 {"B-Kt2" (Tinsley) but the text tallies with the subsequent play - JS}
22. Rdc1 ({I think it is clear that Tinsley's score of this game is nonsense
from hereabouts until later in the game - possibly not his fault as he may
have copied the players' scores - so I have gone out on a limb with my
reconstruction, having to make several emendations along the way. If one
follows Tinsley's score exactly, the following comes about:} 22. Rcc1 {
"QR-Bsq" is clear enough but it runs into trouble before long.} Ba7 23. Bd3 h6
24. Rxc8 Rxc8 25. Nc1 Bd5 26. Be2 {"Kt-K2" but that would leave the b3-pawn en
prise - JS} (26. Ne2 Bb8 27. Bd2 Nxd2 28. Nxd2 Qd6 29. g3 Ba7 30. Ne4 Bxe4 31.
Bxe4 e5 {but now "R-B6" indicates that we have departed from the score by a
wide margin - JS}) 26... Bb8 27. Bd2 Nxd2 28. Nxd2 Qd6 29. g3 Ba7 30. Nc4 {
"Kt-K4" but it makes no sense} Bxc4 31. Bxc4 e5 {and now somehow we have to
find something that looks like "R-B6", which isn't going to happen - JS}) 22...
Ba7 23. Bd3 Rxc2 {Tinsley gives "23...P-KR3 24.RxR RxR" which is legal, but it
transpires that we have to leave the residual black rook on the d-file and the
white rook unopposed on the c-file in order to make a "R-B6" move possible.
The moves as recorded look to me like moves written by a player catching up on
his score after a time scramble and getting them down in the wrong order - JS}
24. Rxc2 h6 {The next few moves now tally with Tinsley's score...} 25. Nc1 Bd5
26. Ne2 {(Now Bxb3 would not be good for Black)} Bb8 27. Bd2 Nxd2 28. Nxd2 Qd6
29. g3 Ba7 30. Ne4 Bxe4 31. Bxe4 e5 32. Rc6 {The aforementioned "R-B6" move,
which now makes perfect sense - JS} Qe7 33. Bc2 Bxd4 34. Nxd4 {"BxKt" - the
first scoring mistake for a number of moves, but almost certainly Nxd4.} Rxd4
35. Rc8+ Ne8 36. Qf3 Rd8 37. Qe4 g6 38. Rxd8 Qxd8 39. Qxe5 {"QxQP"} Nf6 40. Qc5
Kg7 41. Qc4 Qd5 42. Qxd5 Nxd5 43. Kf1 Kf6 44. Ke2 Ke5 45. Kd3 Nb6 46. Ke3 Nd7
47. f4+ Kd5 48. Bd3 Nb6 49. h4 Kc5 50. Ke4 Nd7 51. Bb5 Nf6+ 52. Kd3 Nh5 53. Be8
f6 54. f5 Nxg3 55. fxg6 Nf5 56. Bd7 Ng7 57. Ke4 Kd6 58. Bg4 Kc5 59. Be6 Nxe6
60. Kf5 Kd6 61. Kxf6 Kd5 62. g7 Nxg7 63. Kxg7 Kd4 64. Kxh6 Kc3 65. Kg5 Kxb3 66.
h5 {"K-R5" but clearly a mistake in the score} Kxa4 67. h6 b3 68. h7 b2 69.
h8=Q b1=Q 70. Qd4+ {"Q-Q5ch" - a final scoring mistake. The text move is
perhaps the most likely move. Game end. Source: Tinsley notebooks.} 1/2-1/2

